STEP 8  FINAL ANSWER TEXT (generated from the computed values above)
================================================================================================
Hypothesis (data-grounded): In the Psychology replication project (project == 'rpp'), 40.0% of replicated studies (36 of n = 90 usable replication attempts, after dropping the 7 rows flagged drop == True; 95% Wilson interval 30.5-50.3%) were led by a junior professor - i.e. their most senior author held the rank Assistant Professor (22) or Assistant (14) - so junior faculty ran roughly two in five of the RPP replications. Restricting to the studies that actually replicated (replicated == 1) raises the share to 51.6% (16/31), and the unfiltered 97-row version gives 39.2% (38/97). The number is sensitive to how 'junior professor' is read, since the label is not in the data: Assistant only = 15.6%, Assistant + Assistant Professor (headline) = 40.0%, adding Associate Professor = 66.7%. Two caveats: seniority is coded only for the MOST SENIOR author of each team, so this is the share of studies whose most senior author was junior, not a share of individual authors; and the same field on the original-study side (seniority.o) contains no junior-professor titles in rpp (Professor 76, Associate Professor 9, Researcher 5 of 90), so the answer describes the people who RAN the replications. Supporting, descriptive-only context: replication teams were far less senior than the originals they tested (mean author citations 463 vs 2957), and junior-led attempts replicated at 44.4% vs 27.8% for others - a pattern in n = 90 studies (cells as small as 14) and not evidence of any effect of seniority on replication success.

